<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isidore of Seville</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 560–636</span> · Also known as Saint Isidore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isidore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> was archbishop of Seville from about 600 to 636 and the leading scholar of Visigothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. His Etymologies, an encyclopedia of ancient learning, was one of the most widely read books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and he also wrote a history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>. His relics were taken to León in 1063, where the church of San Isidoro was dedicated to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville.jpg" alt="Isidore of Seville" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bartolomé Esteban Murillo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isidor_von_Sevilla.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
